1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:05,646
Hi — this is a small product-intelligence agent I built for the Kensai take-home.

2
00:00:05,596 --> 00:00:21,768
The problem it solves is the one you described: eight messy, conflicting documents about three supplements, and the job is to turn them into clean intelligence you can actually trust — where every answer traces back to an exact source, conflicts are flagged, and it never guesses.

3
00:00:21,768 --> 00:00:30,740
On the right is the structured intelligence table — product, attribute, value, the source it came from, a confidence level, and a flag.

4
00:00:30,740 --> 00:00:33,409
On the left is the chat box.

5
00:00:33,409 --> 00:00:38,734
Let me show you the detail first, because that's really the point of this exercise.

6
00:00:38,734 --> 00:00:41,755
Here's Vitamin B-twelve for MoleQlar.

7
00:00:41,755 --> 00:00:47,589
The datasheet says twenty-five micrograms; the Amazon listing says five hundred.

8
00:00:47,589 --> 00:01:02,055
The agent doesn't average them or quietly pick one — it shows both with their exact snippets, marks the datasheet value canonical because it's higher-authority and internally consistent at a thousand percent of the reference intake, and raises a conflict flag.

9
00:01:02,055 --> 00:01:04,451
And here's the flip side.

10
00:01:04,451 --> 00:01:11,039
Ritual's Vitamin D reads twenty-five micrograms in one source and a thousand I-U in another.

11
00:01:11,039 --> 00:01:15,740
Those are the same value — so it deliberately does not flag a conflict.

12
00:01:15,740 --> 00:01:18,943
Getting the units right cuts both ways.

13
00:01:18,943 --> 00:01:22,315
Down here it auto-surfaced ten issues.

14
00:01:22,315 --> 00:01:32,550
The non-compliant claim you seeded — Ritual's U-K listing says it "boosts your immune system", which the health-claims reference explicitly lists as not-permitted wording.

15
00:01:32,550 --> 00:01:34,867
The B-twelve conflict.

16
00:01:34,867 --> 00:01:41,013
Wellwoman tagged "suitable for vegans" at one retailer, even though its capsule is gelatin.

17
00:01:41,013 --> 00:01:45,870
Values that simply aren't in the documents, reported as "not found".

18
00:01:45,870 --> 00:01:50,922
And data-quality notes on the O-C-R'd datasheet and the undated blog.

19
00:01:50,922 --> 00:01:53,591
Let me ask it a couple of things.

20
00:01:53,591 --> 00:01:56,521
Is Wellwoman suitable for vegans?

21
00:01:56,521 --> 00:02:02,094
No — because of the gelatin capsule, and it flags the retailer that got it wrong.

22
00:02:02,094 --> 00:02:05,780
How much B-twelve is in one MoleQlar capsule?

23
00:02:05,780 --> 00:02:09,906
It gives the conflict, both figures, both sources.

24
00:02:09,906 --> 00:02:16,195
And notice the trace under each answer — detect, retrieve, reconcile, answer.

25
00:02:16,195 --> 00:02:20,167
It works in steps and tools, not a single prompt.

26
00:02:20,167 --> 00:02:29,320
Quickly, on how it's built: four tools — extract, check-compliance, reconcile, and retrieve — orchestrated in the agent.

27
00:02:29,320 --> 00:02:41,859
The decision I care most about is that the extraction and the conflict-detection are deterministic, not the language model — because on regulatory data you never want a model silently turning a microgram into a milligram.

28
00:02:41,859 --> 00:02:46,182
The model is only used to phrase an answer from evidence it's handed.

29
00:02:46,182 --> 00:02:59,125
Last thing — at ten thousand documents, the in-memory search and re-embedding on start-up break first; I'd move to a proper vector index, like Faiss or Postgres pgvector, with incremental embedding.

30
00:02:59,125 --> 00:03:04,503
There's a README with one-command run steps, and a four-page write-up in the repo.

31
00:03:04,503 --> 00:03:08,214
Thanks so much — I really enjoyed this one.

